# OpenShift Development and Operations Best Practices

This document provides OpenShift-specific guidelines that extend beyond standard Kubernetes practices, covering OpenShift-unique features, security models, and deployment patterns.

## 1. OpenShift-Specific Components and Features

### 1.1 Core OpenShift Resources

**Routes vs Ingress:**
```yaml
# OpenShift Route (preferred over Ingress in OpenShift)
apiVersion: route.openshift.io/v1
kind: Route
metadata:
  name: my-app-route
spec:
  host: myapp.apps.cluster.example.com
  to:
    kind: Service
    name: my-app-service
  tls:
    termination: edge
    insecureEdgeTerminationPolicy: Redirect
```

**DeploymentConfig vs Deployment:**
```yaml
# OpenShift DeploymentConfig (legacy, prefer Deployment)
apiVersion: apps.openshift.io/v1
kind: DeploymentConfig
metadata:
  name: my-app-dc
spec:
  replicas: 3
  triggers:
    - type: ConfigChange
    - type: ImageChange
      imageChangeParams:
        automatic: true
        containerNames:
          - my-app
        from:
          kind: ImageStreamTag
          name: my-app:latest
```

**BuildConfig for Source-to-Image (S2I):**
```yaml
# Source-to-Image BuildConfig
apiVersion: build.openshift.io/v1
kind: BuildConfig
metadata:
  name: my-app-build
spec:
  source:
    git:
      uri: https://github.com/user/my-app.git
      ref: main
    type: Git
  strategy:
    sourceStrategy:
      from:
        kind: ImageStreamTag
        name: nodejs:16-ubi8
    type: Source
  output:
    to:
      kind: ImageStreamTag
      name: my-app:latest
```

### 1.2 ImageStreams and ImageStreamTags

**ImageStream Management:**
```yaml
# ImageStream for managing container images
apiVersion: image.openshift.io/v1
kind: ImageStream
metadata:
  name: my-app
spec:
  lookupPolicy:
    local: false
  tags:
    - name: latest
      from:
        kind: DockerImage
        name: registry.access.redhat.com/ubi8/nodejs-16:latest
      importPolicy:
        scheduled: true
      referencePolicy:
        type: Source
```

**Best Practices:**
- Use ImageStreams to abstract image references
- Enable automatic imports for base images
- Tag images appropriately (dev, test, prod)
- Use `lookupPolicy.local: true` for faster pod startup

### 1.3 OpenShift Templates

**Template Structure:**
```yaml
apiVersion: template.openshift.io/v1
kind: Template
metadata:
  name: nodejs-app-template
objects:
  - apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    metadata:
      name: ${APP_NAME}
    spec:
      replicas: ${{REPLICA_COUNT}}
      template:
        spec:
          containers:
            - name: ${APP_NAME}
              image: ${IMAGE_NAME}:${IMAGE_TAG}
              env:
                - name: NODE_ENV
                  value: ${NODE_ENV}
parameters:
  - name: APP_NAME
    description: Name of the application
    required: true
  - name: REPLICA_COUNT
    description: Number of replicas
    value: "1"
  - name: IMAGE_NAME
    description: Container image name
    required: true
  - name: IMAGE_TAG
    description: Container image tag
    value: "latest"
  - name: NODE_ENV
    description: Node.js environment
    value: "production"
```

## 2. Security Context Constraints (SCCs)

### 2.1 Understanding SCCs

**Default SCCs:**
- `restricted` - Most restrictive (default for most users)
- `nonroot` - Allows non-root user access
- `anyuid` - Allows any user ID
- `privileged` - Full access (use sparingly)

**Custom SCC Example:**
```yaml
apiVersion: security.openshift.io/v1
kind: SecurityContextConstraints
metadata:
  name: my-app-scc
allowHostDirVolumePlugin: false
allowHostIPC: false
allowHostNetwork: false
allowHostPID: false
allowHostPorts: false
allowPrivilegedContainer: false
allowedCapabilities: []
defaultAddCapabilities: []
fsGroup:
  type: MustRunAs
  ranges:
    - min: 1
      max: 65535
runAsUser:
  type: MustRunAsRange
  uidRangeMin: 1000
  uidRangeMax: 2000
seLinuxContext:
  type: MustRunAs
supplementalGroups:
  type: MustRunAs
  ranges:
    - min: 1
      max: 65535
volumes:
  - configMap
  - downwardAPI
  - emptyDir
  - persistentVolumeClaim
  - projected
  - secret
```

**Assigning SCCs:**
```bash
# Add SCC to service account
oc adm policy add-scc-to-user my-app-scc system:serviceaccount:my-namespace:my-service-account

# Add SCC to user
oc adm policy add-scc-to-user anyuid my-user

# Remove SCC from user
oc adm policy remove-scc-from-user anyuid my-user
```

### 2.2 SCC Best Practices

- **Principle of Least Privilege**: Use the most restrictive SCC possible
- **Custom SCCs**: Create custom SCCs rather than using `anyuid` or `privileged`
- **Service Account Binding**: Bind SCCs to service accounts, not users
- **Regular Review**: Regularly audit SCC assignments
- **Documentation**: Document why specific SCCs are needed

## 3. OpenShift CLI (oc) Commands

### 3.1 Essential oc Commands

**Project Management:**
```bash
# Create new project
oc new-project my-project --description="My Project" --display-name="My Project"

# Switch to project
oc project my-project

# Get current project
oc project

# List all projects
oc get projects
```

**Application Deployment:**
```bash
# New app from source code
oc new-app https://github.com/user/my-app.git --name=my-app

# New app from Docker image
oc new-app --docker-image=nginx:latest --name=nginx

# New app from template
oc new-app my-template -p PARAM1=value1 -p PARAM2=value2

# Process template without creating objects
oc process my-template -p PARAM1=value1 --dry-run -o yaml
```

**Build and Deploy:**
```bash
# Start build
oc start-build my-app

# Follow build logs
oc logs -f bc/my-app

# Trigger deployment
oc rollout latest dc/my-app

# Check rollout status
oc rollout status dc/my-app

# Rollback deployment
oc rollback dc/my-app
```

### 3.2 Advanced oc Operations

**Resource Management:**
```bash
# Scale application
oc scale dc/my-app --replicas=3

# Set environment variables
oc set env dc/my-app NODE_ENV=production

# Set resource limits
oc set resources dc/my-app --limits=cpu=500m,memory=512Mi --requests=cpu=100m,memory=128Mi

# Add volume
oc set volume dc/my-app --add --name=data --type=pvc --claim-name=my-pvc --mount-path=/data
```

**Debugging:**
```bash
# Debug pod
oc debug pod/my-app-pod

# Remote shell into pod
oc rsh pod/my-app-pod

# Port forwarding
oc port-forward pod/my-app-pod 8080:8080

# Copy files
oc cp my-file.txt my-app-pod:/tmp/
oc cp my-app-pod:/tmp/my-file.txt ./my-file.txt
```

## 4. OpenShift Operators and OperatorHub

### 4.1 Installing Operators

**From OperatorHub:**
```yaml
# Subscription to install operator
apiVersion: operators.coreos.com/v1alpha1
kind: Subscription
metadata:
  name: my-operator
  namespace: my-namespace
spec:
  channel: stable
  name: my-operator
  source: community-operators
  sourceNamespace: openshift-marketplace
```

**Custom Operator:**
```yaml
# Custom Resource Definition
apiVersion: apiextensions.k8s.io/v1
kind: CustomResourceDefinition
metadata:
  name: myapps.example.com
spec:
  group: example.com
  versions:
    - name: v1
      served: true
      storage: true
      schema:
        openAPIV3Schema:
          type: object
          properties:
            spec:
              type: object
              properties:
                size:
                  type: integer
                image:
                  type: string
  scope: Namespaced
  names:
    plural: myapps
    singular: myapp
    kind: MyApp
```

### 4.2 Operator Best Practices

- **Use OperatorHub**: Prefer operators from OperatorHub when available
- **Namespace Scoping**: Use namespace-scoped operators when possible
- **Version Management**: Pin operator versions in production
- **Monitoring**: Monitor operator health and performance
- **Backup**: Backup custom resources and operator configurations

## 5. OpenShift Networking

### 5.1 Network Policies

**Default Deny Policy:**
```yaml
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: default-deny-all
spec:
  podSelector: {}
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
    - Egress
```

**Allow Specific Traffic:**
```yaml
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-frontend-to-backend
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: backend
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
    - from:
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: frontend
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 8080
```

### 5.2 OpenShift SDN

**Network Configuration:**
```bash
# Check network configuration
oc get network.config.openshift.io cluster -o yaml

# Get cluster network
oc get clusternetwork

# Check node subnets
oc get hostsubnets
```

## 6. OpenShift Storage

### 6.1 Storage Classes

**Dynamic Provisioning:**
```yaml
apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1
kind: StorageClass
metadata:
  name: fast-ssd
provisioner: kubernetes.io/aws-ebs
parameters:
  type: gp2
  fsType: ext4
allowVolumeExpansion: true
reclaimPolicy: Delete
```

### 6.2 Persistent Volume Claims

**PVC with Storage Class:**
```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: my-app-data
spec:
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteOnce
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 10Gi
  storageClassName: fast-ssd
```

## 7. OpenShift Monitoring and Logging

### 7.1 Built-in Monitoring

**ServiceMonitor for Prometheus:**
```yaml
apiVersion: monitoring.coreos.com/v1
kind: ServiceMonitor
metadata:
  name: my-app-metrics
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: my-app
  endpoints:
    - port: metrics
      interval: 30s
      path: /metrics
```

**PrometheusRule for Alerts:**
```yaml
apiVersion: monitoring.coreos.com/v1
kind: PrometheusRule
metadata:
  name: my-app-alerts
spec:
  groups:
    - name: my-app.rules
      rules:
        - alert: HighErrorRate
          expr: rate(http_requests_total{status=~"5.."}[5m]) > 0.1
          labels:
            severity: warning
          annotations:
            summary: High error rate detected
```

### 7.2 Cluster Logging

**Log Collection:**
```bash
# View logs from multiple containers
oc logs -f deployment/my-app --all-containers=true

# View logs with timestamps
oc logs deployment/my-app --timestamps=true

# View previous container logs
oc logs deployment/my-app --previous=true
```

## 8. OpenShift CI/CD with Pipelines

### 8.1 Tekton Pipelines

**Pipeline Definition:**
```yaml
apiVersion: tekton.dev/v1beta1
kind: Pipeline
metadata:
  name: build-and-deploy
spec:
  params:
    - name: git-url
      type: string
    - name: image-name
      type: string
  tasks:
    - name: git-clone
      taskRef:
        name: git-clone
      params:
        - name: url
          value: $(params.git-url)
    - name: build-image
      taskRef:
        name: buildah
      runAfter:
        - git-clone
      params:
        - name: IMAGE
          value: $(params.image-name)
```

**PipelineRun:**
```yaml
apiVersion: tekton.dev/v1beta1
kind: PipelineRun
metadata:
  name: build-and-deploy-run
spec:
  pipelineRef:
    name: build-and-deploy
  params:
    - name: git-url
      value: https://github.com/user/my-app.git
    - name: image-name
      value: my-registry/my-app:latest
```

## Best Practices Summary

### OpenShift-Specific Guidelines

1. **Use Routes over Ingress** for external traffic management
2. **Prefer Deployments over DeploymentConfigs** for new applications
3. **Leverage ImageStreams** for image lifecycle management
4. **Apply Least Privilege** with custom SCCs
5. **Use oc CLI** for OpenShift-specific operations
6. **Monitor with built-in tools** (Prometheus, Grafana, AlertManager)
7. **Implement CI/CD** with OpenShift Pipelines (Tekton)
8. **Plan for multi-tenancy** with proper project isolation

### Migration from Kubernetes

- **Routes**: Replace Ingress resources with OpenShift Routes
- **Security**: Understand SCC requirements vs Pod Security Policies
- **Storage**: Verify storage class compatibility
- **Networking**: Test network policies with OpenShift SDN
- **Monitoring**: Adapt monitoring to OpenShift's built-in stack
- **CLI**: Learn oc commands that extend kubectl functionality

By following these OpenShift-specific practices alongside standard Kubernetes guidelines, you'll effectively leverage OpenShift's enhanced capabilities while maintaining compatibility with the broader Kubernetes ecosystem.
